the first student that we actually had  was actually about 50 yards away from  where we're standing right now which is  kind of a weird full-circle moment for  eSports it was basically one big open  space and we used curtains to partition  the teams from each other from the  broadcast desk I mean the entire  production crew couldn't have been more  than 25 at that time but we were so  proud of it you know we were able to  launch you know the first regular  eSports league in the West out of that  studio and then we moved on and you know  we went to the Manhattan Beach studios  the year after it was actually a movie  theater soundstage so we would have  build out a much bigger stage have the  teams actually in desks kind of like you  see here we're able to bring in an  audience for the first time and then the  year after that we actually moved to  this location so we've been able to  build out this space a little bit but  this is kind of what we feel is at this  point kind of the best broadcast we want  to put on this is you know what we're  able to really highlight the pros give  really great analysis in between the  games build out the right content pieces  we want to build and deliver our great  experiences fans  the na LCS or the North American League  of Legends Championship Series  represents the best League of Legends  teams in North America every weekend the  crowds show up in real life to enjoy the  games and hundreds of thousands more  fans tune in online the popularity and  success of the LCS broadcast is raising  the bar  today we're going behind the scenes at  the LCS arena to see what it takes to  put on one of the best productions and  competitive gaming on any particular  gameday we've got about a hundred and  fifty people who are participating in  the show and then there's probably  another 20 to 30 people who are working  behind the scenes who are not here on  the weekends but are doing all the prep  work to make sure that everything  everything actually happens everything  that happens someone had to do that what  camera are we watching okay well someone  told someone to grab that camera someone  switched to that camera someone's  Manning that camera cool to grab my  strobe okay someone made that graphic  someone wrote what was on that graphic  so when research was on that graphic and  someone put the button put that guy  Africa on camera  okay cool within one scene eight people  done stuff I am an eSports interviewer  and League of Legends expert I guess you  could say I've been covering the space  since 2011 it is crazy to see how hard  everyone at the event is working the  casters and the production crew work  days in advance to make sure that  they've got like the right show at the  beginning the storylines that they're  telling are honest ones to have stats  and interesting stuff to bring up that  really help the audience understand like  who is the underdog in any scenario or  who is the advantage team when the game  is live everything's improv everything's  off-the-cuff we're talking and  describing what we see but the little  intro bits we at least have bullet  points where we're bringing up things  like hey here's what you want to look at  this team's got this record right now  and here's what's going on with this  will always be slacking each other  saying hey what are you gonna talk about  this game what do we want to get out of  this game as a whole oh you want to  touch on double of stats I got you there  we got your full D sheets and whatnot  that you look up and say okay we'll have  this this this and this happens then  those are tie breakers for our game days  we'll all meet up at the CLG office and  then on the van ride over it's really  quiet  well I'll be listening to music and then  I always have like a ritual like going  to the bathroom and I'll just like kind  of like meditate and like relax in there  for like 10 to 15 minutes if I can wake  up I can listen to some jazz some calm  music know I have an hour to myself do a  few you know monolith  I mean they they because they  immediately you see Mauna LOA's I'm  gonna lose ooh do all the funny stuff  you would never do in front of somebody  else like face and you go at it and come  in shout with all my friends all my  co-workers if we have read through that  day we do read through make sure that  rings you know in place do our normal  rehearsals make sure everything works  log in the tournament row make sure you  expect at a game from there typically  we'll go behind the scenes we'll go to  the catering area which is where they  bring in they've got food for everybody  behind the scene and then after that we  go to everybody goes to makeup everybody  goes to wardrobe  I start with awfully for the guys are  still in rehearsal but they always end  early so as soon as they get out they  come check in here  some people want kind of a distraction  for a minute while they're here because  it's kind of a little break from from  prep and others just want to get back to  that prep as soon as possible but if  they're nervous and more excited and  talkative then we'll like joke around  with them sometimes I'm joking around  with Haunter and sometimes he he has his  ear buds in and he said he's in the zone  and darshan will come in and sometimes  he has his ear buds in too but other  times we can get him to sing for us  cause he likes to sing a lot I think  something interesting audience doesn't  know is that the make up room is like  right below the fans so like while we're  like getting makeup done and getting  ready for the game I can hear like the  casters and the fans like right above us  like going crazy something fans at home  probably only get a small glimpse of is  the the pro player lounge we have a  space where teams can go during the  games before the game is right after the  games it's a great space for them to be  able to congregate and sometimes you get  really good interactions between pro  players who are coming in and grab  catering or a snack or a drink or walk  through to go to the bathroom and you  forget that they're you know maybe  friends or kind of had their own funny  inside jokes so watching them kind of  pass by each other and their  interactions has always been kind of a  really cool thing for me one area that  no one ever gets to see besides the  media really in the pros I guess is the  press room which is upstairs at the LCS  a lot of fans will see the stairs and  you wonder kind of where'd those head -  it's the press room up there there's  interview rooms up there work room  everybody's watching the games and then  we  really lucky to have kind of like a  skybox style window where you can look  out on the arena and see everything  that's happening when we load into the  LCS games beforehand we're sitting in a  lobby we're all 10 players and all 20  spectators and just like in a normal  game of League of Legends when you have  10 players sitting around in a lobby  with nothing to do for five minutes  because they're waiting on it to start  they all start typing sometimes they'll  just randomly like talk trash to each  other usually jokingly and sometimes  they just do weird asterisk  conversations where they say things like  walks over and Pat's you on the head and  weird stuff like that and more often  than not I end up getting a laugh out of  those at least once a day I definitely  didn't think that this is what I would  end up doing but I couldn't have asked  for anything better I get to do makeup  and I get to travel the world which is  all I've ever wanted and it realized  that it would happen  mostly with men and in eSports but I  love it because the energy here is just  so great I still love this game after  all these years I love that I get to  come in and talk about it with excited  fans or interviews about it all that fun  stuff and it's it's just really exciting  getting to still be part of something so  amazing my best vision of the NA LCS  production is that our people continue  to get better they continue to strive so  that the production is better week two  than it was week one better week three  than it was week two and just continue  that absolute hunger that what they're  doing is is never as good as it could be  and we're never kind of hitting the  ceiling in in production quality only  the best teams in North America get a  chance to play on the world stage in the  next episode of this series we're  telling the story of the most ambitious  eSport event on the planet and what it  means for the players striving to get  there  [Music]

